# tools/c/convergence_prove_witness.rish -- one prover where twenty-seven hand-written cases were owed.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/c/convergence_prove_witness.rish
#
# `tools/c/convergence_census.sh` measured 30 tools writing to the tracked tree with 3 proving they
# converge. The obvious repair was to hand-write a convergence case into each of the other 27
# controls -- which is the census's own finding performed once per tool: a rule kept where a hand
# remembered. **22 of the 27 share one invocation shape**, a single file path, so one prover answers
# for all of them and the five that differ are named rather than skipped in silence.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES is narrow and is the tree's own claim: running a writer twice on a copy leaves the
# file byte-identical to what one run left -- `infusion(world') -> world'`.
#
# THE LAST TWO VERDICTS ARE THE ONES THAT MATTER. `inert` says the FIRST run changed nothing
# anywhere in the pen, so the sample exercised no path and the reading proves nothing. A sample
# that triggers no work is the quiet way a convergence proof lies, and the control plants exactly
# that case.
#
# `wrote_beside` is what `inert` was carrying without saying (`20260910`): the run left the subject
# byte-identical and wrote SOMEWHERE ELSE. The prover compared the subject alone and invoked the
# tool at its path in the real tree, so a tool resolving its own root from `$0` wrote into the
# caller's checkout and the reading came back `inert`. Both halves are pressed by the control --
# the write is seen, and the tool is copied into the pen so it cannot reach out -- and both are
# proven against a mutated prover that fails them.

say "convergence-prove: Language EN -- one instrument for a property twenty-seven tools claim."
say "convergence-prove: Style Gauge, Meter -- four verdicts, and the two honest ones are named."
say "convergence-prove: Lens TAME -- safety first: the subject is a copy, never the tree."

say "convergence-prove: the control -- thirty-two behaviors, including a tool built to diverge, two second-run refusals, a tool written in Rishi, and a tool that writes beside its subject..."
let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/c/convergence_prove_control.sh"]
assert ctl.ok else "convergence-prove: the control refused --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"
assert ctl.out contains "pass=32 fail=0" else "convergence-prove: the control did not prove thirty-two behaviors --\n${ctl.out}\n${ctl.err}"

# invariant: the control exercises the prover against a TRACKED writer on a real sample, not only
# against tools it invented, and asserts the reading is `converges` rather than `inert` -- a green
# built on an inert sample is the lie that verdict exists to prevent.
assert ctl.out contains "coverage: a tracked tree writer was exercised on a real sample, and both second-run refusals -- one holding the file, one writing before it refused -- were told apart" else "convergence-prove: the control never exercised a tracked writer"

# invariant: the beside legs are asserted by their own coverage line, so a prover that stopped
# looking outside the subject -- or stopped copying the tool into the pen -- reds here rather than
# reading green over a tool writing into the caller's tree.
assert ctl.out contains "coverage: a beside-write was shown on the first run and on the second, the pen held a root-resolving tool where the elder shape let it out, and a prover with the comparison defeated fails four of these six" else "convergence-prove: the control never pressed the beside legs"

say "GREEN: convergence-prove -- a writer run twice inside a pen that holds the tool as well as the sample, with divergence caught, both second-run refusals told apart, an inert sample refused as proof, and a beside-write named rather than called inert."
